+{"timestamp":"2026-08-28T11:41:00Z","branch":"full_target","note":"Candidate theorem: n=1 is the only solution exponent. Existence: (a,b,c)=(1,2,2). For n>=3, reduce a hypothetical solution to gcd(a,b,c)=1. Mod 2 gives b even. Since n>=2, 3b^n is divisible by 4, so 2a^n=4c^n-3b^n is divisible by 4; hence a even. For n>=3, both 2a^n and 3b^n are divisible by 8, so 4c^n is divisible by 8 and c is even, contradicting primitiveness. For n=2, modulo 3 gives c^2 congruent to 2a^2. Squares mod 3 are 0,1, so a,c are divisible by 3; substituting a=3a1,c=3c1 yields b^2=3(4c1^2-6a1^2), hence b divisible by 3, again contradicting primitiveness. No external theorem is required."}

